<h2>How the matter reached the Sessions Court</h2>
<p>The wife had filed a complaint under Section 12 of the Protection of Women from Domestic Violence Act, 2005, alongside criminal proceedings under Sections 498A/406/34 IPC. The Magistrate granted interim maintenance under Section 23. The husband did not pay. After the arrears mounted, the trial court struck off his defence. He appealed against both that order and the dismissal of his application seeking relief from the arrears, contending that his employment had ended in August 2019 and that medical difficulties disabled him from earning.</p>

<h2>What the appellate court held</h2>
<p>The Sessions Court dismissed both appeals. Three strands of reasoning stand out.</p>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Purpose of interim maintenance.</strong> Interim maintenance exists to provide immediate, temporary financial support and protection to the aggrieved woman during the pendency of the case. Allowing a respondent to litigate while starving the applicant of that support defeats the provision.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Arrears do not evaporate.</strong> The husband remained liable for arrears that accrued before his employment ended. Later incapacity, even if genuine, does not wash out amounts that fell due while he had the means to pay.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Last resort, but a real one.</strong> Applying <em>Rajnesh v. Neha</em>, the court confirmed that striking off the defence is a permissible response to non-compliance with a maintenance order — reserved for wilful default, after opportunity to comply, but fully available when that threshold is met.</div>
</div>

<h2>The enforcement ladder in maintenance matters</h2>
<p>Delhi practice now follows a recognisable escalation when maintenance orders are ignored:</p>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>1. Demand and computation.</strong> The applicant places a tabulated arrears calculation on record and seeks enforcement.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>2. Show cause and opportunity.</strong> The court requires the respondent to explain the default and gives time to clear or substantially reduce arrears.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>3. Coercive recovery.</strong> Warrants of attachment, recovery as fines, and in appropriate cases detention, on the pattern applicable to orders under Section 144 of the BNSS (formerly Section 125 CrPC).</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>4. Striking off the defence.</strong> Where the default is found deliberate and contumacious despite the earlier steps, the respondent forfeits the right to contest — the measure upheld in this case.</div>
</div>

<h2>Why the decision matters</h2>
<p>Respondents sometimes treat interim maintenance as negotiable — something to be adjusted against future settlement, or ignored while appeals are explored. This judgment shows the risk in that strategy. The right to defend a DV case is not absolute; it coexists with the obligation to obey the court\'s interim directions. A respondent who wants the court\'s ear must first respect the court\'s orders. Conversely, the decision preserves the safeguards: default must be wilful, the measure a last resort, and genuine incapacity — proved, not merely asserted — remains a defence through the modification route under Section 25(2).</p>

<div class="note"><p>Medical or employment difficulties are addressed prospectively by seeking modification with documents, not retrospectively by defaulting and explaining later. The date on which the application for modification is filed usually marks the earliest point from which any reduction can operate.</p></div>

<div class="check">
  <p>Applicants: maintain a clean arrears ledger, and seek striking off only after the court\'s coercive steps have been given a chance — premature requests are refused.</p>
  <p>Respondents: pay what fell due while you earned; contest quantum through appeal or modification, never through silent default.</p>
  <p>Both sides: interim maintenance orders under Section 23 DV Act are appealable under Section 29, but an appeal does not by itself stay the obligation to pay.</p>
</div>

<p>The appeals were dismissed and the trial court\'s orders affirmed. This article describes the decision for general information; it is not legal advice on any individual case.</p>
